$$SYNTAX-LEVEL 2
*(mgdb-history
<meta name="twitter:card" content="summary">
<meta name="twitter:site" content="@MaizeGDB">
<meta property="og:url" content="https://maizegdb.org/maize_history">
<meta property="og:title" content="History of Maize Genetics &amp; MaizeGDB Timelines | Community Milestones">
<meta property="og:description" content="Explore over a century of maize genetics discoveries, landmark research papers, community cooperation milestones, and the evolution of the MaizeGDB database infrastructure.">

<main class="mgdb-page mgdb-hub-page mgdb-history-page" id="history-top">
  <nav class="mgdb-breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
    <a href="/">Home</a><span aria-hidden="true">&rsaquo;</span><span aria-current="page">Maize History &amp; Timelines</span>
  </nav>

  <!-- HERO SECTION -->
  <section class="mgdb-hero" aria-labelledby="history-title">
    <div class="mgdb-hero-emblem">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 96 96" role="img" aria-label="Maize history hourglass and DNA icon">
        <!-- Hourglass and DNA milestone motif -->
        <circle cx="48" cy="48" r="38" fill="#e8f4ec" stroke="#235c37" stroke-width="3" />
        <!-- Clock / Timeline ticks -->
        <line x1="48" y1="16" x2="48" y2="24" stroke="#235c37" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round" />
        <line x1="48" y1="72" x2="48" y2="80" stroke="#235c37" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round" />
        <line x1="16" y1="48" x2="24" y2="48" stroke="#235c37" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round" />
        <line x1="72" y1="48" x2="80" y2="48" stroke="#235c37" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round" />
        <!-- Clock hands -->
        <path d="M48 48 L48 28 M48 48 L64 48" stroke="#d99a0b" stroke-width="4" stroke-linecap="round" />
        <circle cx="48" cy="48" r="6" fill="#235c37" />
      </svg>
      <strong>Community<br />History</strong>
    </div>
    <div class="mgdb-hero-body">
      <p class="mgdb-hero-stamp"><span aria-hidden="true">&#9679;</span> Updated&#58; $(data_date)</p>
      <h1 id="history-title">Maize genetics community history</h1>
      <p class="mgdb-hero-description">How a letter between three researchers in 1929 became a worldwide community&#58; its meetings, its organizations, its database, and a century of maize genetics behind them.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- STICKY SECTION TABS -->
  <nav class="mgdb-section-tabs" aria-label="Sections on this page">
    <a href="#history-origins" class="is-current">Origins</a>
    <a href="#history-timeline">Timeline</a>
    <a href="#history-community">The community</a>
    <a href="#history-mgec">The MGEC</a>
    <a href="#history-maizegdb">MaizeGDB</a>
    <a href="#history-classic-reads">Classic papers</a>
    <a href="#history-galleries">Galleries</a>
    <a href="#history-domestication">Domestication</a>
    <a href="#history-overview">Metrics</a>
    <a href="#history-related">Related resources</a>
  </nav>

  <!-- SECTION 1: INTERACTIVE TIMELINE -->
  <!-- Origins. Drawn from /cooperators, which tells this story in prose the
       group wrote; the photograph and the people links are that page's. -->
  <section id="history-origins" aria-labelledby="history-origins-heading">
    <div class="mgdb-section-heading">
      <div><h2 id="history-origins-heading" class="mgdb-panel-title">Origins</h2></div>
    </div>

    <div class="history-origins-layout">
      <figure class="history-origins-figure">
        <img src="/images/ithaca.gif" alt="Maize researchers photographed in Ithaca, New York in 1929" loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>
          Ithaca, New York, 1929. In the foreground, <a href="/person?id=12822">George Beadle</a> and his dog Toto;
          behind him, left to right, <a href="/person?id=12839">C. R. Burnham</a>,
          <a href="/person?id=13011">Marcus Rhoades</a>, <a href="/person?id=12877">Rollins Emerson</a>,
          and <a href="/person?id=12966">Barbara McClintock</a>.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="history-origins-copy">
        <h3>The spirit of maize cooperation</h3>
        <p>The maize research community has a long history of cooperation. It began in the 1920s, when
        researchers held &ldquo;cornfabs&rdquo; to share ideas. In 1929 Rollins A. Emerson, his student
        George Beadle, and Professor Allan C. Fraser sent a letter sharing published and unpublished
        maize linkage data.</p>
        <p>That single act of data sharing grew into a worldwide community of thousands of maize
        cooperators, and into the free public resources and leadership committees that organize the
        work today.</p>

        <div class="history-origins-define">
          <h4>What is a maize cooperator?</h4>
          <p>Someone who shares an interest in maize genetics, genomics, and breeding research, and who
          embraces collaboration by sharing published and unpublished materials, data, and ideas.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="history-timeline" aria-labelledby="history-timeline-heading">
    <div class="mgdb-section-heading">
      <div><h2 id="history-timeline-heading" class="mgdb-panel-title">Timeline</h2></div>
    </div>
    <div class="history-filter-bar">
      <div class="history-filter-group" role="tablist" aria-label="Timeline category filter">
        <button class="history-filter-btn is-active" type="button" data-filter="all">All Milestones <span class="history-filter-count">$(total_events)</span></button>
        <button class="history-filter-btn" type="button" data-filter="breakthrough"><span class="filter-dot filter-dot-breakthrough" aria-hidden="true"></span>Research Breakthroughs <span class="history-filter-count">$(breakthrough_count)</span></button>
        <button class="history-filter-btn" type="button" data-filter="meeting"><span class="filter-dot filter-dot-meeting" aria-hidden="true"></span>Meetings &amp; Conferences <span class="history-filter-count">$(meeting_count)</span></button>
        <button class="history-filter-btn" type="button" data-filter="cooperative"><span class="filter-dot filter-dot-cooperative" aria-hidden="true"></span>Cooperative Resources <span class="history-filter-count">$(coop_count)</span></button>
      </div>
      <div class="history-search-box">
        <label for="history-search" class="mgdb-visually-hidden">Search timeline milestones</label>
        <input class="mgdb-input history-search-input" id="history-search" type="search" placeholder="Search milestone, author, or year..." autocomplete="off">
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="timeline-container">
      <div class="timeline-track-line" aria-hidden="true"></div>
      $(timeline_events)
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- How the community organizes itself. The MGC is current and its business
       lives on maizegenetics.org; the MGEC is closed and this is the record of
       it. Both are summarized here rather than linked away to a legacy page. -->
  <section id="history-community" aria-labelledby="history-community-heading">
    <div class="mgdb-section-heading">
      <div><h2 id="history-community-heading" class="mgdb-panel-title">The community</h2></div>
    </div>

    <div class="history-org-grid">

      <article class="history-org-card history-org-current">
        <span class="history-org-era">2019 &ndash; present</span>
        <h3>Maize Genetics Cooperation</h3>
        <p>In 2019 the Maize Genetics Executive Committee voted to incorporate as a 501&#40;c&#41;&#40;3&#41;
        non-profit, the Maize Genetics Cooperation &#40;MGC&#41;. Its purpose is to advance the interests of
        people who research maize genetics, genomics, and breeding, and to train the next generation
        of scientists.</p>
        <p>An elected Board of Directors manages the MGC and coordinates its committees&#58; the Meeting
        Steering Committee, the Advocacy Committee, the Awards and Nominations Committee, and the
        Membership Committee.</p>
        <p>Current MGC business &mdash; the board, the committees, the awards, the code of conduct,
        and membership &mdash; is on the MGC&rsquo;s own site.</p>
        <!-- One destination, so one link: the membership call to action points at
             maizegenetics.org, which is also where the card's other link went. -->
        <p class="history-org-links">
          <a href="https\://www.maizegenetics.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Become a MGC member <span aria-hidden="true">&nearr;</span></a>
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="history-org-card history-org-closed">
        <span class="history-org-era">2000 &ndash; 2019</span>
        <h3>Maize Genetics Executive Committee</h3>
        <p>Ten members elected by the whole community, speaking for maize genetics to funding
        agencies, to growers, and to the wider life sciences. It ceased activity once the MGC was
        formed, and its record is kept.</p>
        <p class="history-org-links"><a href="#history-mgec">About the MGEC <span aria-hidden="true">&darr;</span></a></p>
      </article>

      <article class="history-org-card">
        <span class="history-org-era">Since 1959</span>
        <h3>The annual meeting</h3>
        <p>Twelve maize geneticists met at Allerton Park, Illinois in 1959. The meeting has run every
        year since, in six countries, and twice online. It is organized by the MGC&rsquo;s Meeting
        Steering Committee.</p>
        <p class="history-org-links"><a href="/maize_meeting/">Maize Genetics Meeting <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span></a></p>
      </article>

      <article class="history-org-card">
        <span class="history-org-era">Since 1932</span>
        <h3>The newsletter and the stock center</h3>
        <p>Two of the community&rsquo;s oldest shared resources&#58; the Maize Genetics Cooperation
        Newsletter, which has carried working notes and unpublished observations since 1932, and the
        Maize Genetics Stock Center, which has distributed seed since 1953.</p>
        <p class="history-org-links"><a href="/mnl">Newsletter archive <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span></a></p>
      </article>

    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 2: MAIZEGDB EVOLUTION & REDESIGNS -->
  <section id="history-mgec" aria-labelledby="history-mgec-heading">
    <div class="mgdb-section-heading">
      <div><h2 id="history-mgec-heading" class="mgdb-panel-title">The MGEC</h2></div>
    </div>

    <div class="history-mgec-layout">
      <div class="mgdb-prose">
        <blockquote class="history-mgec-quote">
          <p>It is the mission of the Maize Genetics Executive Committee to identify both the needs
          and the opportunities for maize genetics, and to communicate this information to the
          broadest possible life science community. This community includes scientists, funding
          sources for scientists, and the end users for the accomplishments of maize genetics, from
          farmers to consumers.</p>
          <cite>Jeff Bennetzen, Maize Genetics Cooperation Newsletter 75</cite>
        </blockquote>
        <p>The committee grew out of an ad hoc vision committee Ed Coe formed in 1999, and the first
        ten members were elected in 2000 on more than 300 votes. Members served five-year terms, two
        rotating off each year, with appointed seats added so that Europe, Asia, Africa, Latin America
        and small colleges were represented.</p>
        <p>What it did was argue the community&rsquo;s case&#58; visits to Washington that preceded the
        RFPs for sequencing the maize genome, an agreement with industry that released more than two
        million maize ESTs to public-sector researchers, community surveys sent to funding agencies,
        and the meeting with NSF in 2010 that led to the Maize Genome Assembly and Annotation
        Consortium.</p>
        <p>In 2019 it voted to establish the Maize Genetics Cooperation and stood down.</p>
      </div>

      <aside class="mgdb-aside-card history-mgec-card">
        <h3>The record</h3>
        <dl class="history-mgec-figures">
          <div><dt>Terms</dt><dd>$(mgec_terms)</dd></div>
          <div><dt>People who served</dt><dd>$(mgec_members)</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Activity reports</dt><dd>$(mgec_activities)</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Documents</dt><dd>$(mgec_documents)</dd></div>
        </dl>
        <a class="mgdb-button mgdb-button-secondary" href="/mgec">Open the MGEC record</a>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="history-maizegdb" aria-labelledby="history-maizegdb-heading">
    <h2 id="history-maizegdb-heading" class="mgdb-panel-title">MaizeGDB</h2>
    <p class="mgdb-panel-lead">The history of community database informatics, from the inception of MaizeDB in 1991 to the modern MaizeGDB pan-genomics ecosystem.</p>

    <div class="history-maizegdb-grid">
      <div class="history-maizegdb-narrative">
        <h3>From MaizeDB to MaizeGDB</h3>
        <p>In 1991, the USDA-ARS and the University of Missouri launched <strong>MaizeDB</strong>, the first centralized digital genetic database for maize. In 2003, the project transitioned into <strong>MaizeGDB</strong> \(Maize Genetics and Genomics Database\) hosted by the USDA-ARS Corn Insects and Crop Genetics Research Unit and Iowa State University.</p>

        <p>Over three decades, MaizeGDB has expanded from genetic linkage maps and seed stocks to chromosome-scale reference genomes, pan-genomic matrices, structural variant catalogs, transcriptomics atlases, and AI-ready biological tools.</p>

        <div class="history-pillars">
          <div class="pillar-card">
            <h4>Operational Evolution</h4>
            <ul>
              <li>Transforming into an open community data portal</li>
              <li>Interoperability with Ensembl Plants, NCBI, and Gramene</li>
              <li>High-throughput variant calling &amp; pan-genome indexing</li>
              <li>FAIR data principles with AgBioData consortium</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div class="pillar-card">
            <h4>Functional Evolution</h4>
            <ul>
              <li>Instant autocomplete search and REST API endpoints</li>
              <li>Genome browsers \(JBrowse, GBrowse\) with multi-track curation</li>
              <li>Specialized tools: qTeller, CornCyc, PanEffect, FETA</li>
              <li>Comprehensive stock inventory and phenotypic catalogs</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div class="pillar-card">
            <h4>Interface Evolution</h4>
            <ul>
              <li>Unified responsive layouts and accessible color schemes</li>
              <li>Hierarchical section navigation with sticky tab scrollspy</li>
              <li>Standardized reference cards with 1-click DOI/PMID copy</li>
              <li>Optimized modern typography and fast page delivery</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="history-conference-links">
          <strong>Redesign Archive &bull; 2011 Maize Genetics Conference&#58;</strong>
          <div class="history-links-row">
            <a class="mgdb-button mgdb-button-sm mgdb-button-secondary" href="http://ftp.maizegdb.org/maizemeeting/2011/T16_mm2011_andorf.ppt" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2011 Meeting Presentation \(PPT\) &#8599;</a>
            <a class="mgdb-button mgdb-button-sm mgdb-button-secondary" href="http://ftp.maizegdb.org/maizemeeting/2011/P19_2011Poster.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2011 Meeting Poster \(PDF\) &#8599;</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="history-diagrams-column">
        <figure class="history-diagram-card">
          <img src="/images/maizegdb_timeline.jpg" alt="Timeline of MaizeGDB" loading="lazy">
          <figcaption>Timeline of MaizeGDB &bull; Evolution from MaizeDB to MaizeGDB</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <figure class="history-diagram-card">
          <img src="/images/interface_timeline.jpg" alt="Timeline of MaizeGDB Interface Redesign" loading="lazy">
          <figcaption>Timeline of MaizeGDB Interface Redesign Milestones</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <figure class="history-diagram-card">
          <img src="/images/coop_timeline.png" alt="Maize Genetics Cooperation Timeline" loading="lazy">
          <figcaption>Maize Genetics Cooperation Timeline &bull; Decades of Community Resources</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 3: CLASSIC MAIZE PAPERS -->
  <section id="history-classic-reads" aria-labelledby="history-classic-heading">
    <h2 id="history-classic-heading" class="mgdb-panel-title">Classic papers</h2>
    <p class="mgdb-panel-lead">Landmark foundational papers and seminal publications that shaped early maize genetics, linkage mapping, cytogenetics, and genome structure.</p>

    <div class="classic-reads-grid">
      <!-- 1: East & Hayes 1911 -->
      <article class="classic-read-card">
        <div class="classic-read-year">1911</div>
        <div class="classic-read-body">
          <h3 class="classic-read-title"><a href="/docs/EastHayes1911.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Inheritance in Maize</a></h3>
          <p class="classic-read-authors">E. M. East and H. K. Hayes</p>
          <p class="classic-read-desc">Connecticut Agricultural Experiment Station Bulletin No. 167. One of the earliest comprehensive genetic inheritance studies in maize.</p>
          <a class="mgdb-button mgdb-button-sm mgdb-button-secondary" href="/docs/EastHayes1911.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Download PDF &#8599;</a>
        </div>
      </article>

      <!-- 2: Hayes & East 1911 -->
      <article class="classic-read-card">
        <div class="classic-read-year">1911</div>
        <div class="classic-read-body">
          <h3 class="classic-read-title"><a href="/docs/HayesEast1911.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Improvement in Corn</a></h3>
          <p class="classic-read-authors">H. K. Hayes and E. M. East</p>
          <p class="classic-read-desc">Connecticut Agricultural Experiment Station Bulletin No. 168. Pioneering breeding methods and inbred line evaluation.</p>
          <a class="mgdb-button mgdb-button-sm mgdb-button-secondary" href="/docs/HayesEast1911.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Download PDF &#8599;</a>
        </div>
      </article>

      <!-- 3: Emerson, Beadle, Fraser 1935 -->
      <article class="classic-read-card">
        <div class="classic-read-year">1935</div>
        <div class="classic-read-body">
          <h3 class="classic-read-title"><a href="/docs/EmersonBeadleFraser1935.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">A Summary of Linkage Studies in Maize: Memoir</a></h3>
          <p class="classic-read-authors">R. A. Emerson, G. W. Beadle, and A. C. Fraser</p>
          <p class="classic-read-desc">Cornell University Agricultural Experiment Station Memoir 180. The foundational compendium of all known maize linkage groups and genetic maps.</p>
          <a class="mgdb-button mgdb-button-sm mgdb-button-secondary" href="/docs/EmersonBeadleFraser1935.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Download PDF &#8599;</a>
        </div>
      </article>

      <!-- 4: Longley 1961 -->
      <article class="classic-read-card">
        <div class="classic-read-year">1961</div>
        <div class="classic-read-body">
          <h3 class="classic-read-title"><a href="/docs/Longley61.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Translocations and Inversions in Maize</a></h3>
          <p class="classic-read-authors">A. E. Longley</p>
          <p class="classic-read-desc">USDA-ARS Agricultural Research Service Technical Bulletin. Cytological analysis of chromosomal rearrangements \(PDF 37.8 MB\).</p>
          <a class="mgdb-button mgdb-button-sm mgdb-button-secondary" href="/docs/Longley61.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Download PDF \(37.8 MB\) &#8599;</a>
        </div>
      </article>

      <!-- 5: Stadler Symposia 1969-1983 -->
      <article class="classic-read-card">
        <div class="classic-read-year">1969&ndash;1983</div>
        <div class="classic-read-body">
          <h3 class="classic-read-title"><a href="https://mospace.umsystem.edu/xmlui/handle/10355/66509" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Papers from the Stadler Symposia</a></h3>
          <p class="classic-read-authors">University of Missouri Agricultural Experiment Station</p>
          <p class="classic-read-desc">Complete digital collection of presentations from the prestigious annual Stadler Genetics Symposia held at the University of Missouri.</p>
          <a class="mgdb-button mgdb-button-sm mgdb-button-secondary" href="https://mospace.umsystem.edu/xmlui/handle/10355/66509" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View MOspace Archive &#8599;</a>
        </div>
      </article>

      <!-- 6: Kato Yamakake 1975 -->
      <article class="classic-read-card">
        <div class="classic-read-year">1975</div>
        <div class="classic-read-body">
          <h3 class="classic-read-title"><a href="/docs/Kato_1975_Cytological Studies of Maize.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cytological Studies of Maize and Teosinte in Relation to Their Origin and Evolution</a></h3>
          <p class="classic-read-authors">Takeo Angel Kato Yamakake</p>
          <p class="classic-read-desc">Doctoral dissertation documenting chromosome knob morphology and cytological relationships across American maize and teosinte races.</p>
          <a class="mgdb-button mgdb-button-sm mgdb-button-secondary" href="/docs/Kato_1975_Cytological Studies of Maize.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Download PDF &#8599;</a>
        </div>
      </article>

      <!-- 7: Burnham 1980 -->
      <article class="classic-read-card">
        <div class="classic-read-year">1980</div>
        <div class="classic-read-body">
          <h3 class="classic-read-title"><a href="/docs/ancillary/burnham/index.html">Discussions in Cytogenetics</a></h3>
          <p class="classic-read-authors">C. R. Burnham</p>
          <p class="classic-read-desc">Sixth printing of Burnham's seminal textbook covering structural chromosome variations, translocations, aneuploidy, and inversions.</p>
          <a class="mgdb-button mgdb-button-sm mgdb-button-secondary" href="/docs/ancillary/burnham/index.html">Read Online &#8599;</a>
        </div>
      </article>

      <!-- 8: Sheridan 1982 -->
      <article class="classic-read-card">
        <div class="classic-read-year">1982</div>
        <div class="classic-read-body">
          <h3 class="classic-read-title"><a href="/docs/Maize_For_Biological_Research.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Maize for Biological Research</a></h3>
          <p class="classic-read-authors">Dr. William F. Sheridan \(Ed.\)</p>
          <p class="classic-read-desc">Plant Molecular Biology Association handbook covering maize developmental genetics, tissue culture, mutagenesis, and cytogenetics protocols.</p>
          <a class="mgdb-button mgdb-button-sm mgdb-button-secondary" href="/docs/Maize_For_Biological_Research.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Download PDF &#8599;</a>
        </div>
      </article>

      <!-- 9: Bennett 1995 -->
      <article class="classic-read-card">
        <div class="classic-read-year">1995</div>
        <div class="classic-read-body">
          <h3 class="classic-read-title"><a href="/docs/ancillary/zmdnaamt.html">Nuclear and Chromosomal DNA Amounts in Zea mays</a></h3>
          <p class="classic-read-authors">Michael D. Bennett</p>
          <p class="classic-read-desc">Detailed compilations of nuclear DNA C-values, flow cytometry measurements, and genome size estimations across maize inbreds and landraces.</p>
          <a class="mgdb-button mgdb-button-sm mgdb-button-secondary" href="/docs/ancillary/zmdnaamt.html">Read Online &#8599;</a>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 4: HISTORIC IMAGE GALLERIES -->
  <section id="history-galleries" aria-labelledby="history-galleries-heading">
    <h2 id="history-galleries-heading" class="mgdb-panel-title">Galleries</h2>
    <p class="mgdb-panel-lead">Archival photographic records documenting historical maize genetics conferences, pioneering researchers, and community gatherings.</p>

    <div class="history-galleries-grid">
      <article class="history-gallery-card">
        <div class="history-gallery-badge">1975 Symposium</div>
        <h3 class="history-gallery-title"><a href="/community/image_gallery/IMS1975">1975 International Maize Symposium</a></h3>
        <p class="history-gallery-location">Urbana, Illinois &bull; University of Illinois</p>
        <p class="history-gallery-desc">Historical photos compiled by Marty Sachs, with participant names and affiliations identified by Ed Coe and colleagues.</p>
        <a class="mgdb-button mgdb-button-sm mgdb-button-primary" href="/community/image_gallery/IMS1975">View 1975 Gallery &#8594;</a>
      </article>

      <article class="history-gallery-card">
        <div class="history-gallery-badge">2010 Conference</div>
        <h3 class="history-gallery-title"><a href="/community/image_gallery/MM2010">2010 Maize Genetics Conference</a></h3>
        <p class="history-gallery-location">Riva del Garda, Italy &bull; 52nd Annual Meeting</p>
        <p class="history-gallery-desc">Photo collection from the historic 2010 European Maize Genetics Conference held in Riva del Garda, Italy.</p>
        <a class="mgdb-button mgdb-button-sm mgdb-button-primary" href="/community/image_gallery/MM2010">View 2010 Gallery &#8594;</a>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 5: DOMESTICATION & ORIGINS -->
  <section id="history-domestication" aria-labelledby="history-domestication-heading">
    <h2 id="history-domestication-heading" class="mgdb-panel-title">Domestication</h2>
    <p class="mgdb-panel-lead">The 9,000-year evolutionary journey of maize from its wild progenitor teosinte in southwestern Mexico to a global agricultural staple.</p>

    <div class="history-domestication-content">
      <div class="domestication-card">
        <h3>From Balsas Teosinte to Modern Corn</h3>
        <p>Maize \(<i>Zea mays</i> ssp. <i>mays</i>\) was domesticated approximately 9,000 years ago from the wild grass teosinte \(<i>Zea mays</i> ssp. <i>parviglumis</i>\) in the lowland Balsas River basin of southwestern Mexico. Indigenous agriculturalists selected for pivotal morphological changes, including non-shattering ears, naked kernels, single unbranched stalks, and apical dominance driven by key regulatory loci like <i>tb1</i> \(<i>teosinte branched1</i>\) and <i>tga1</i> \(<i>teosinte glume architecture1</i>\).</p>
        
        <p>As maize diversified across North, Central, and South America, it adapted to extreme elevations, photoperiods, and climates, generating thousands of traditional landraces before giving rise to modern commercial dent, flint, sweet, and popcorn varieties.</p>
        
        <div class="domestication-links">
          <a class="mgdb-button mgdb-button-secondary" href="https://rilab.ucdavis.edu/maize_history.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Explore Maize Domestication History \(Ross-Ibarra Lab, UC Davis\) &#8599;</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

